 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** The server delivers committed revisions over WebSocket connections it hosts itself. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for adding repeated reads and seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets give low-latency updates with no realtime-vendor dependency, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect handling — a cost accepted to keep the board independent of a realtime provider. This shapes the three downstream nodes: the change store needs replayable revisions and reliable publication, delivery manages sessions and publishes, and browser sync reconnects and catches up.
